WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.150
All right, next up, if you're a writer,

2
00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:04.270
but not quite as prolific as Stephen King,

3
00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:05.820
we might have a new way for you

4
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.820
to get those tipping and typing fingers going.

5
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:11.020
Thrillist reports there's a new cafe in Japan

6
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.670
designed just for writers.

7
00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:15.710
They got free wifi, plenty of outlets to plug in,

8
00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:18.810
bottomless coffee, but there's one teeny tiny catch,

9
00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.345 line:15%
you are not allowed to leave until you finish writing.

10
00:00:21.345 --> 00:00:22.280 line:15%
All right?

11
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.420 line:15%
And you'll be charged $1.20 for every half an hour

12
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.860 line:15%
that you need extra motivation and you stay there.

13
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.350 line:15%
Yeah, you can only leave after you've finished

14
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:32.030 line:15%
your writing goal for the day,

15
00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:34.307 line:15%
or when the cafe closes at 7:00, whatever comes first.

16
00:00:34.307 --> 00:00:35.410 line:15%
(audience laughs)

17
00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:37.040 line:15%
I will say, I've written a couple books,

18
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:37.873 line:15%
you've written a couple books.

19
00:00:37.873 --> 00:00:40.950 line:15%
It is so difficult to do because you are your own boss.

20
00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.890 line:15%
It's on you to sit down and do it and clock in and finish.

21
00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:47.560
And I'm like the best boss, because I would sit down

22
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:49.880
and be like, boss, can I have a five minute break?

23
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.230
And I'd be like, yes, you can.

24
00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.074
And then I'd go.

25
00:00:52.074 --> 00:00:53.732
(Drew laughs)

26
00:00:53.732 --> 00:00:54.990
So you have to have such structure.

27
00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.560
And what you think of this cafe?

28
00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:57.393
I think it's great

29
00:00:57.393 --> 00:00:59.450
because it gives you the structure you need.

30
00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:02.600
When you're artists, you're not used to a 9:00 to 5:00.

31
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.710
And so this gives you a reason to get in there, stay,

32
00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:06.920
and a goal to finish.

33
00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.320
Structure and accountability.

34
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.153
Raise of hands

35
00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:15.323
who thinks this idea is good and should come stateside?

36
00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:17.810
I double with you, ma'am.

37
00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:18.960
What is your name?

38
00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:19.793
Caroline.

39
00:01:19.793 --> 00:01:23.280
Caroline, I am so with you with the double hands.

40
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.990
Out of curiosity, why are we enthusiastically

41
00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:26.823
putting up two hands?

42
00:01:26.823 --> 00:01:29.210
I know why for me, but why for you?

43
00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:30.760
Because you need that accountability

44
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.150
to just get stuff done.

45
00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:34.546
I'm with you Ross, I'll do the five minute break.

46
00:01:34.546 --> 00:01:35.457
I'm like, yeah.
Totally.

47
00:01:35.457 --> 00:01:38.020
Sometimes I give myself a 10 minute break too,

48
00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.120
you know what I mean, whatever I need.

49
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.390
Why not make some microwave salmon while you're at it?

50
00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:43.403
Well, no.
Caroline.

51
00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:44.612
(audience claps)

52
00:01:44.612 --> 00:01:47.027
It wasn't bad, but I'm not saying that.

53
00:01:47.027 --> 00:01:50.410
Caroline, in true writing fashion and form,

54
00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.587
you just paid the earlier story off, thank you very much.

55
00:01:52.587 --> 00:01:55.540
Yeah, thank you for that bookend, thank you.

56
00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.370
I love this idea so much

57
00:01:57.370 --> 00:01:59.150
and whatever company is doing this,

58
00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.430
please come all over the world.

59
00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:02.580
I just love this.

60
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.950
This a great story to me.

61
00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:05.783
Do it.

62
00:02:05.783 --> 00:02:06.800
Totally.
I want more.

63
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:07.835
I'm writing a third book.

64
00:02:07.835 --> 00:02:08.668
(Drew gasps)

65
00:02:08.668 --> 00:02:09.590
I know, I know, and I'm like-

66
00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.880
Rossy, whoa, we haven't talked about this.

67
00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.307
(audience cheers)
(audience applauds)

68
00:02:13.307 --> 00:02:16.826
I'm writing, I'm riding around.

69
00:02:16.826 --> 00:02:17.810
It's a little cookbook.

70
00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.240
I'm working on Rossipes, yes.

71
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:20.593
Ah!
I know.

72
00:02:20.593 --> 00:02:21.990
Oh my god.

73
00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:22.934
I know, is that exciting?

74
00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:23.898
Well...

75
00:02:23.898 --> 00:02:26.590
(audience cheers)
(audience applauds)

76
00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.020
I'm working on it now, is that exciting?

77
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:30.760
This is...

78
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.200
The world is right with the Rossipes book,

79
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.220
'cause that's been a thing for you for a long time,

80
00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.053
it's due.

81
00:02:36.053 --> 00:02:37.840
It's how I lost the 60 pounds,

82
00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.220
I kept it off cooking for myself

83
00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:40.978
and really trying to figure out food

84
00:02:40.978 --> 00:02:42.800
and be healthy and be good.

85
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.050
But I will tell you, as inspired as I am to write this book,

86
00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.600
I am like, mm, I need a break.

87
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.820
So this is, I need this, I needed to hear this story today.

88
00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.340
So thank you, Drew, for bringing this one to me.

89
00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.540
I'm glad you liked it.
Thank you.

90
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.130
Oh, and here's one I have for you.

91
00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:57.440
We have a little woodworker in England

92
00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.290
making a really big difference.

93
00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:00.190
I love this story.

94
00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:03.020
People reports 12-year-old Gabriel Clark

95
00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.990
has always loved woodworking.

96
00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:06.840
And he's a hit on Instagram,

97
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.740
selling handmade cutting boards and bowls

98
00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:11.970
to save up money to buy a bike.

99
00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:13.200
How great is that?

100
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.060 line:15%
But now he's doing something else amazing.

101
00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:18.780 line:15%
He's raffling off a special custom bowl

102
00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.450 line:15%
for Ukrainian children.

103
00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:21.480 line:15%
Yeah.

104
00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.081 line:15%
And proceeds, we love this,

105
00:03:23.081 --> 00:03:25.860 line:15%
they're gonna go to Save the Children.

106
00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.813 line:15%
And get this, Gabriel has already raised $327,000.

107
00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.355 line:15%
That's incredible.
Woodworking.

108
00:03:35.355 --> 00:03:36.979 line:15%
(audience applauds)

109
00:03:36.979 --> 00:03:38.653 line:15%
Wow.
It's just incredible.

110
00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.290 line:15%
He is such a lovely human being.

111
00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:43.820
I think his work is really beautiful.

112
00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.160
Beautiful, yeah.

113
00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:49.950
And what a heart and soul in this beautiful human being.

114
00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:52.560
And speaking of supporting Ukraine,

115
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.220
we wanna shout out to our partners at Pluto TV.

116
00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.360
Actually, you can catch us on Pluto TV anytime

117
00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.690
on The Drew Barrymore Show channel.

118
00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:05.930
And you can check out their Ads for Change campaign

119
00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:08.250
because they're running free ads to get the support

120
00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.750
for incredible organizations

121
00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:12.870
doing work on the ground in Ukraine.

122
00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:16.687
Save the Children, World Central Kitchen, UNHCR,

123
00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.640
and the International Rescue Committee.

124
00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.550
So if you wanna help and you're looking for a place to start

125
00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.370
because that's a huge part of it,

126
00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:26.100 line:15%
is just where do I start?

127
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.220 line:15%
These are great groups to go and support,

128
00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:30.450 line:15%
and you can get all the information on our website.

129
00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:34.490 line:15%
And I always say too, like, $1, $5, that's it.

130
00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.230
One of these places, it will make a difference.

131
00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:37.737
Absolutely, yeah.
Thank you so much.

132
00:04:37.737 --> 00:04:40.085
Thank you, Pluto, we love you.

133
